---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import speaking from '../../content/assets/about/speaking.jpg';
import SocialLinks from '../components/SocialLinks.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { person, withContext } from '../lib/schema';

const description =
  'Piotr Lewandowski is a Product Architect at Dynatrace in Gdańsk, working on frontend architecture, observability and practical automation.';
---

<Base title="About" {description} jsonLd={withContext({ '@type': 'ProfilePage', mainEntity: person(Astro.site!) })}>
  <article class="post about">
    <header>
      <h1>Hi, I’m Piotr</h1>
    </header>
    <Image src={speaking} width={570} alt="Piotr speaking at a meetup" class="cover" loading="eager" />
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I’m a Product Architect at <a href="https://www.dynatrace.com/">Dynatrace</a>, based in Gdańsk, Poland. I’ve
        spent more than ten years building web applications. I started on the backend in the Java ecosystem, moved to
        the frontend with JavaScript and TypeScript, and stayed for the architecture: how large applications are
        structured, built and kept healthy once they’re in production.
      </p>
      <p>
        Along the way I’ve supported products shipped both as SaaS and on-premise, and run long-term migrations of
        software that had to keep working for customers the whole time.
      </p>

      <h2>What I work on</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Frontend architecture.</strong> Structure of large TypeScript codebases, monorepos and build tooling,
          and the trade-offs that come with each choice, including undoing the ones that didn’t pay off.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Observability.</strong> What happens to an application after release: monitoring, incidents and what
          they teach about the design.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Practical automation and AI workflows.</strong> Small tools built like production code, and AI agents
          used where they save real time, with a human kept in front of anything irreversible.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Speaking, writing and mentoring</h2>
      <p>
        I give deep-dive talks from one developer to others, internally and at meetups. I used to organise PonadProgram,
        a meetup that showed IT students what the job looks like in practice.
      </p>
      <p>
        My articles on Medium, inDepth and dev.to have more than 100,000 views together, and some were translated into
        Polish and Japanese. I also mentor other engineers.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        The best way to reach me is <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/piotr--lewandowski/">LinkedIn</a>. You can also
        write to <a href="mailto:mail@piotrl.net">mail@piotrl.net</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <footer>
      <h2>Find me on:</h2>
      <SocialLinks />
    </footer>
  </article>
</Base>
